<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pedro Paterno</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1857–1911</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pedro Paterno was a Filipino lawyer, writer and politician from a wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> family. He mediated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pact-of-biak-na-bato">Pact of Biak-na-Bato</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the revolutionaries in 1897, then presided over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malolos">Malolos</a> Congress and served as prime minister of the First Philippine Republic in 1899. His novel Ninay (1885) is often counted as the first novel by a Filipino.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pedro-paterno.jpg" alt="Pedro Paterno" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anthony R. Tuohy, suplemento español del The Far Eastern Review, Manila I.F. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_Paterno.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
